@@ -0,0 +1,15 @@
+# Tournament mode
+
+Choose Tournament beside New Game, select 4 or 8 players, enter their names and select Start tournament. Entry order sets seeds. Four players meet as 1–4 and 2–3. Eight players meet as 1–8, 4–5, 3–6 and 2–7. Names use NFC, trimmed ends and single spaces between words, with 2–20 Unicode code points and no duplicates ignoring case.
+
+Start turn reveals a fresh board for the named player. Both players in a match use the same deal, with a different code for each match. Quarterfinals use 4x4, semifinals 6x6 and the final 8x8. Fewer moves wins, then lower active time, then the higher seed. Next turn hands the device to the second player; Next match advances the bracket. The final result opens the Champion screen. Leave tournament asks before discarding unfinished progress.
+
+Tournament play shares the board, matching rules and session clock with the normal game. It pauses the normal game and leaves saved board size and best scores unchanged. Hidden documents and confirmation dialogs add no active time. Brackets and past tournaments use captioned tables, winners include a text marker, and screen changes focus the screen heading. All announcements use the status paragraph in Game.tsx.
+
+Completed brackets are stored newest first under `np3:tournaments` as `{ "v": 3, "items": [...] }`, with at most ten records. Invalid saved data is ignored until the next save replaces it. If storage is blocked, completed records remain available for the current page session and the Past tournaments area explains that progress cannot be saved.
+
+Every tournament screen has Export tournament and Load tournament controls beside a Tournament JSON box. The export format is `neon-pairs/tournament`, version 3. Object keys are sorted recursively. The `state` contains the phase, setup draft, current match index, tournament and current player's game snapshot. The snapshot preserves fractional active milliseconds and any remaining mismatch time. Setup exports retain entered names and player count. Invalid names block export or import with the same validation messages as starting a tournament. Empty setup fields represent names not yet entered.
+
+Loading validates the complete schedule, board sizes, distinct board codes, seed progression, results, winners and current game before replacing state. Unknown fields are reported by name. Replacing unfinished work opens the native leave dialog; Keep playing and Escape preserve current progress. Completed imports also appear in history, without duplicating an identical record.
+
+The pure reducer and tournament rules live in `src/game/knockout/rules.ts`. Its neighboring unit tests cover both brackets, tie-breaking, normalized names, import validation and storage behavior. `tests/knockout.e2e.test.ts` plays complete four- and eight-player tournaments in Chromium, including each card activation, and verifies history after reload, focus, target sizes, JSON handling and normal preferences.
